---
import ComparisonTable from "@components/article/ComparisonTable.astro";
import FAQSection from "@components/article/FAQSection.astro";
import RelatedArticles from "@components/article/RelatedArticles.astro";
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
import { getArticleBySlug } from "../../data/articles";

// Get article metadata from central data file
const article = getArticleBySlug("best-travel-insurance-remote-workers", "blog")!;
const { title, description, readingTime, image, imageAlt } = article;
const pubDate = new Date(article.pubDate);

// Table of contents
const headings = [
  { text: "I Spent $2,400 on a Hospital", slug: "introduction", depth: 2 },
  { text: "The Options at a Glance", slug: "quick-comparison", depth: 2 },
  { text: "What You Actually Need", slug: "what-to-look-for", depth: 2 },
  { text: "My Actual Opinions", slug: "reviews", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Stuff NOT Covered", slug: "coverage", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Quick Questions", slug: "faq", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Just Pick One", slug: "conclusion", depth: 2 },
];

// Comparison table
const comparisonColumns = [
  { key: "provider", label: "Provider", width: "120px" },
  { key: "price", label: "Cost/Month" },
  { key: "medical", label: "Medical" },
  { key: "electronics", label: "Electronics" },
  { key: "bestFor", label: "Who It's For" },
];

const comparisonRows = [
  {
    provider: "<strong>SafetyWing</strong>",
    price: "~$63 per 4 weeks",
    medical: "$250K",
    electronics: "Add separately",
    bestFor: "Most nomads",
  },
  {
    provider: "<strong>World Nomads</strong>",
    price: "Per-trip quote",
    medical: "Up to $1M",
    electronics: "Limited",
    bestFor: "Adventure stuff",
  },
  {
    provider: "<strong>Genki</strong>",
    price: "From €52",
    medical: "€1M+",
    electronics: "Add separately",
    bestFor: "Europe people",
  },
  {
    provider: "<strong>Passport Card</strong>",
    price: "Quote",
    medical: "$1M",
    electronics: "Included",
    bestFor: "Want the best",
  },
  {
    provider: "<strong>Insured Nomads</strong>",
    price: "Quote",
    medical: "$1M+",
    electronics: "Add separately",
    bestFor: "Americans",
  },
];

// FAQ data
const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Can I work and still be covered?",
    answer: "<p>With nomad insurance, yes. With regular travel insurance, usually no.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Pre-existing conditions?",
    answer:
      "<p>Mostly no. Some cover sudden worsening. For ongoing stuff you need real expat health insurance.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Already left home. Can I still buy it?",
    answer:
      "<p>SafetyWing and Genki let you buy from anywhere. Traditional insurance usually requires buying before you leave.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "How do claims work?",
    answer:
      "<p>Small stuff: pay yourself, submit receipts, get reimbursed. Big stuff: better providers bill the hospital directly. You don't pay anything upfront.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Does SafetyWing cover me when I visit home?",
    answer:
      "<p>A little. The Essential plan covers up to 30 days of medical care in your home country. Going home for longer? You'll need something else for that stretch.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Genki Traveler or Genki Native?",
    answer:
      "<p>Traveler is travel medical insurance, cheaper and fine for a year or two on the road. Native is real long-term health insurance with a one-year minimum. If you've basically moved abroad, Native.</p>",
  },
];

// Related articles
const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: "Top Destinations for Digital Nomads",
    excerpt: "Cities with great WiFi, affordable living, and thriving communities.",
    href: "/blog/top-destinations-digital-nomads/",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1528181304800-259b08848526-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Digital nomad destination",
  },
  {
    title: "Essential Travel Tech for Nomads",
    excerpt: "Protect your gear investment with the right insurance and accessories.",
    href: "/blog/essential-travel-tech-nomads/",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1519389950473-47ba0277781c-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Travel tech setup",
  },
  {
    title: "Best Banking for Digital Nomads",
    excerpt: "Manage your finances across borders without excessive fees.",
    href: "/blog/best-banking-digital-nomads/",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1563986768494-4dee2763ff3f-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Mobile banking app",
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Travel"
    categorySlug="blog"
    subcategorySlug="travel"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">I Spent $2,400 on a Hospital in Vietnam. Cash.</h2>
  <p>
    Food poisoning. Three days hooked up to an IV in Hanoi. Tests, medication, the whole thing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Didn't have travel insurance. Thought I was saving money. Forty bucks a month, right? I'll just be careful.
  </p>
  <p>
    Yeah. That worked out great.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here's the thing though. Regular travel insurance wouldn't have helped anyway. Those policies? Built for families
    doing two weeks in Cancun. Not for someone bouncing between countries with no return ticket, <a href="/guides/productivity/">working
    from random cafes</a>, zero clue when "home" even happens.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most policies literally exclude "working." Typing on your laptop counts. They cap trips at 30-90 days, then expect
    you back in your home country. Which... okay. That's not how any of this works.
  </p>
  <p>
    So what actually covers us?
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Short version:</strong> SafetyWing. I've had it eighteen months now. It's about $63 every four weeks if
    you're under 40, it renews on its own, and it doesn't care that you work from a laptop.
  </p>
  <p>
    Want the longer explanation? That's below.
  </p>

  <!-- Quick Comparison -->
  <h2 id="quick-comparison">The Options at a Glance</h2>

  <ComparisonTable columns={comparisonColumns} rows={comparisonRows}/>

  <!-- What to Look For -->
  <h2 id="what-to-look-for">What You Actually Need to Check</h2>

  <h3>Does It Cover Working?</h3>
  <p>
    This one catches people. You're in Bali, <a href="/guides/productivity/">working from a cafe</a>, you slip and break
    your wrist. Normal travel
    insurance? "Sorry, you were conducting business activities. Excluded."
  </p>
  <p>
    Nomad insurance doesn't care. Work all you want.
  </p>

  <h3>How Much Medical Coverage?</h3>
  <p>
    US hospitals charge ten grand a night. Not joking. Even in "cheap" countries, anything serious gets expensive fast.
  </p>
  <p>
    I wouldn't go below $100K coverage. $250K is better. Some people want a million. Up to you.
  </p>

  <h3>Emergency Evacuation</h3>
  <p>
    A friend's appendix burst in rural Thailand. Middle of nowhere. Needed to get to Bangkok, then home.
  </p>
  <p>
    Evacuation cost: $80,000.
  </p>
  <p>
    Insurance covered it. If he didn't have insurance? I don't know. Sell a kidney maybe.
  </p>
  <p>
    This stuff happens. Get the evacuation coverage.
  </p>

  <h3>Trip Length Limits</h3>
  <p>
    Normal insurance makes you go home after 30-90 days. Nomad insurance lets you renew monthly. Forever. Much better.
  </p>

  <h3>Electronics</h3>
  <p>
    Your laptop dies, you need a new one today. Not next week. Today.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some policies cover electronics. Some don't. Some have tiny limits like $500. Check this.
  </p>

  <!-- Top Providers Reviewed -->
  <p>
    Quick warning before the details. Every one of these got repriced in the last year or so, some more than once.
    Treat the numbers here as a starting point and get your own quote. Age and where you're going change everything.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reviews">My Actual Opinions on Each One</h2>

  <h3 id="safetywing">SafetyWing</h3>
  <p>
    This is what I have. Eighteen months now.
  </p>
  <p>
    It's... fine? Like, genuinely fine. Nothing exciting. It just works. You pay monthly, it renews automatically, you
    can sign up from anywhere. If something happens, you file a claim. Never had issues.
  </p>
  <p><strong>The numbers:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>$62.72 per 4 weeks for ages 18 to 39 (the plan got an update in February 2026)</li>
    <li>$250,000 max for medical</li>
    <li>Up to 30 days of medical coverage back in your home country</li>
    <li>No pre-existing conditions, maternity or cancer treatment on the Essential plan</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>What's good:</strong> Cheap. Simple. Made for nomads.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What sucks:</strong> Lower limits than fancy options. Electronics not included. You gotta buy that
    separately. US coverage is weird and limited.
  </p>
  <p>
    But for the price? Hard to beat.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/go/safetywing" rel="nofollow sponsored" class="text-teal-ink underline decoration-2 underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" target="_blank">
      Get SafetyWing Quote &rarr;
    </a>
  </p>

  <h3 id="worldnomads">World Nomads</h3>
  <p>
    Okay so if you're actually going to DO stuff. Diving, skiing, rock climbing, motorbike rentals. This is the one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most insurance sees "adventure activities" and runs away. World Nomads covers 250+ activities. I used
    them for a month in Indonesia when I was diving every day.
  </p>
  <p><strong>The numbers:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Priced per trip, so get a quote for your dates</li>
    <li>Single trips up to 180 days on the US plans</li>
    <li>12 months max per policy, extensions included</li>
    <li>Covers the fun stuff</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>What's good:</strong> Adventure coverage.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What sucks:</strong> Expensive. And a hard ceiling. A year per policy, max. Not built for people who've
    stopped counting.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/go/world-nomads" rel="nofollow sponsored" class="text-teal-ink underline decoration-2 underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" target="_blank">
      Get World Nomads Quote &rarr;
    </a>
  </p>

  <h3 id="genki">Genki</h3>
  <p>
    German company. Really good for Europe. Their old Explorer plan is gone. It's Genki Traveler now, and Genki
    Native if you want full long-term health insurance.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you're doing the <a href="/guides/digital-nomad-visa-portugal/">Portugal digital nomad visa</a> thing, or spending
    serious time in the EU, this might be what you
    need. It meets all the visa requirements.
  </p>
  <p><strong>The numbers:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Traveler from €52/month, depends on age, deductible and region</li>
    <li>Native from €180/month</li>
    <li>Meets <a href="/guides/digital-nomad-visa-spain/">Schengen visa</a> requirements</li>
    <li>Coverage in the US and Canada is limited on both</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>What's good:</strong> Cheap. Europe-focused. Visa compliant.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What sucks:</strong> It's really built for Europe. Coverage elsewhere exists but... it's not the focus. And the US is
    basically emergency-only.
  </p>

  <h3 id="passport">Passport Card</h3>
  <p>
    The expensive one.
  </p>
  <p>
    High limits on everything. Electronics covered. Dental covered. They have an app that actually works. Direct billing
    at hospitals so you don't pay upfront.
  </p>
  <p><strong>The numbers:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Prices depend a lot on the plan, so get a quote</li>
    <li>Direct payment at the hospital with the card</li>
    <li>Higher limits than the budget options</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>What's good:</strong> Best coverage. Direct hospital billing. No hassle claims.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What sucks:</strong> Three to five times the price of SafetyWing. Overkill for most people.
  </p>

  <h3 id="insured-nomads">Insured Nomads</h3>
  <p>
    Built for Americans dealing with... American healthcare stuff.
  </p>
  <p>
    US coverage is better than the others. Works with ACA requirements. Has telemedicine.
  </p>
  <p><strong>The numbers:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>World Explorer covers single trips from 7 to 364 days</li>
    <li>You pick the max benefit ($250K, $1M or higher)</li>
    <li>Better US coverage than most</li>
    <li>24/7 doctor calls included</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>What's good:</strong> Actually works in America.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What sucks:</strong> Only really matters if you're American and need US coverage.
  </p>

  <!-- Coverage Deep Dive -->
  <h2 id="coverage">Stuff That's NOT Covered</h2>
  <p>
    Just so you know:
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pre-existing conditions.</strong> Basically never covered. Some policies cover sudden worsening. But ongoing
    management? Nope.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Routine checkups.</strong> This is emergency insurance. Not regular health insurance.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pregnancy.</strong> Usually excluded after a certain point. Check the specifics.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Extreme sports.</strong> Unless they're specifically listed. Skydiving probably not covered unless you
    bought the adventure plan.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Mental health.</strong> Hit or miss. Some cover it. Many don't. If this matters to you, check specifically.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <h2 id="faq">Quick Questions</h2>
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <!-- Conclusion -->
  <h2 id="conclusion">Just Pick One</h2>
  <p>
    Look. I spent way too long researching this stuff. Compared every plan. Read every review. Made spreadsheets.
  </p>
  <p>
    You know what I learned? They're all basically fine. The differences matter less than just having something.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Most people:</strong> SafetyWing. Cheap, works, done.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Adventure stuff:</strong> World Nomads.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Europe-focused:</strong> Genki.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Want the best:</strong> Passport Card.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>American:</strong> Insured Nomads.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pick one. Sign up. Stop thinking about it.
  </p>

  <h3>The Real Talk</h3>
  <p>
    I know insurance feels like a waste. Nothing bad is going to happen. You're healthy. You're careful.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then you're in a foreign hospital at 3am, no one speaks English, and you're trying to figure out if your credit card
    can handle the bill.
  </p>
  <p>
    My $2,400 in Vietnam? That was minor. Food poisoning. Uncomfortable but not dangerous.
  </p>
  <p>
    I've met people with $30K bills. $50K bills. One guy needed surgery in the Philippines.$70K.
  </p>
  <p>
    SafetyWing costs about $63 every four weeks. That's two mediocre dinners out. Skip them. Get the insurance.
  </p>
  <p>
    Future you will be grateful. Or at least not broke.
  </p>
  <p>
    Planning your trip? Check the <a href="/blog/top-destinations-digital-nomads/">destination guide</a>. Set up <a
      href="/blog/best-banking-digital-nomads/">banking that works internationally</a>. Get the boring stuff sorted
    before you go.
  </p>

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
